WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.620
A $50 monthly gym fee dwarfs the few dollars of steel and motors inside each machine. This is First Principles Daily, Episode 126. A typical gym charges around $50 a month. The steel, motors, and basic electronics that move the weights and belts represent only a small slice of that price. The difference arises because the industry layers real estate leases, high-member turnover, and custom installation on top of the equipment itself. That spread is not fixed by physics.

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:34.020
It is the result of how facilities are cited, equipped, and operated today.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:39.200
The atoms inside a treadmill or power rack remain ordinary commodity materials whose

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.400
market prices have stayed low for decades.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:46.040
The monthly bill a member pays must cover everything else that surrounds those atoms.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:51.700
Most commercial gyms follow a model built around high-volume locations in retail strips or

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:52.220
malls.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:54.840
Rent and build out dominate the budget there.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:01:04.640
Franchise disclosure documents show planet fitness clubs require between 1.28 and 3.77 million dollars to open when equipment is financed.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:08.720
The bulk goes to lease hold improvements and site work rather than the machines.

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:16.800
Equipment itself is purchased in packages that range from 150,000 to 1.8 million dollars for a midsize facility.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:22.240
Those packages already include markups for distribution, installation, and warranties.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.560
Membership churn adds another layer.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:28.680
Industry data indicate roughly one-third of members leave each year.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:32.380
Operators must constantly market and staff to replace them.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:34.700
Those costs are passed along in dues.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:39.060
Each club is still largely assembled on site with conventional HVAC,

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:40.780
flooring, and electrical runs.

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:46.640
There is little repetition that would drive unit costs down the way factory production does for consumer goods.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:50.980
The result is that the price a member pays feels normal inside the industry.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:54.760
The underlying materials and energy remain ordinary commodities.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:59.680
Real estate brokers, general contractors, and equipment distributors each take their

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:01.280
margin on the same set of atoms.

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:06.780
The financing cost of tying up capital during months of permitting and fit-out compounds before

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:08.360
the first dues dollar arrives.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:14.060
If a magic wand could instantly form the finished treadmills, racks, and dumbbells from raw stock,

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:17.520
the material cost would be far lower than the delivered price.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:25.360
Holesale guides list basic strength equipment at roughly $500 to $1500 per station and dumbbells

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:31.740
at $1.5 to $10 per kilogram. Those figures already include fabrication. Stripping away the

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:37.260
fabrication steps leaves commodity steel at a few dollars per kilogram and simple electric motors

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:43.020
at a few hundred dollars each. A rough tally for the steel, copper, and motors in a typical cardio

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:47.740
strength mix might therefore sit in the low thousands of dollars per club rather than

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:50.060
the six-figure equipment packages now quoted.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:54.820
Dividing the finished equipment and build-out costs by that material estimate produces

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:58.440
an idiot index in the range of 10-20 for the hardware alone.

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:03.260
The index climbs much higher once real estate and operating overhead are included.

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:06.000
The gap lives in several concrete places.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:11.580
Leases in high traffic retail corridors add carrying costs that compound through the permitting

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:12.900
and construction timeline.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:18.060
Custom one-off electrical and HVAC work for each site prevents the repetition that would

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:20.200
let contractors amortize designs.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:26.540
Member acquisition and retention programs needed because churn hovers near 33% further

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:29.020
inflate the revenue target that do's must meet.

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:34.340
Energy to run the lights, climate control, and machines is real, but modest next to these

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:34.620
layers.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:39.900
The price therefore reflects accumulated process steps more than the atoms inside the machines.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:45.060
designs. Even if every motor and barbell were delivered at pure commodity cost, the club

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:48.840
would still need to recover the expense of holding a lease while inspectors review

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:53.360
drawings, and while new members are recruited to offset those who cancel after a few months.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:58.820
A redesign would start by moving equipment fabrication towards standardized, high-volume

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:04.500
production, using the same commodity steel and motors already available at low cost. That

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:09.100
step would require manufacturers to accept thinner margins in exchange for volume commitments

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:14.880
from a network of clubs rather than one-off sales, next would come modular interior systems that

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:20.320
reduce onsite build time. Those systems would cut both labor and the financing cost of long

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:25.540
construction schedules. Such systems would need building codes that treat repeatable gym layouts

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:31.480
as standard rather than custom tenant improvements. A third move would involve locating clubs in lower

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:37.680
rent industrial or secondary retail spaces. Operators would accept slightly longer member travel times

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:43.000
and return for lower overhead. The hard constraint is that any such shift must still deliver enough

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:48.280
perceived convenience and variety to keep churn from rising further. Policy changes around

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:53.580
permitting speed and standardized modular approvals would also be necessary before the capital cost

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:59.360
of new clubs could fall toward the material floor. Each of these moves collides with existing incentives.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:05.680
Landlords prefer anchor tenants who sign long leases in prime locations. Contractors earn more

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:10.560
from bespoke work. Equipment suppliers protect margins by selling through dealers rather

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:15.380
than direct high-volume contracts. Overcoming those incentives would require either

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:20.880
a single large operator willing to absorb the initial volume commitment, or regulatory reform

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:25.280
that treats modular fitness interiors like standardized shipping containers rather than

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:30.160
unique construction projects. One principle is that real estate and permitting timelines

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:35.580
act like hidden multipliers on every kilogram of steel. They turn ordinary materials into

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:41.140
expensive monthly dues. Another is that churn forces operators to keep charging for replacement

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:46.200
rather than for the equipment already in place. The first visible sign that someone is testing

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:50.720
this approach would be a new club opening with documented equipment and build costs well

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:56.360
below the current franchise ranges while maintaining comparable member counts. That's a wrap on

00:05:56.360 --> 00:06:00.920
today's first principles breakdown. The idea is travel further when you pass them on.

